  "body": "Stacked on #810 (on #798)\n\nFork synced at v0.17.0.\n\n## What changed\nFollow-ups to the #804 index-swing check, from the review of PR #810.\n\n- **A table A with no axis vector is judged** (`crates/app/src/posting_index_swing.rs`). Before, a work-turning rotary whose `.mch` stated no `axis` vector wasn't judged at all, a silent pass. Now the clause reads the rotary's line from its `axis` vector, or with none, from its RS-274 letter:\n  - **`A`:** about X, judged like the microARC's. The post writes a leaning tool axis as an A turn about X whatever the file says.\n  - **`B` / `C`:** off X, outside this clause, as before.\n  - **No vector and no RS-274 rotary letter** (say `U`): every op is refused by name, asking for the rotary's `axis`. Its line is unknown, so it could be an A under the tool.\n- **Projects saved before #804 are named on load** (`crates/app/src/project/swing_note.rs`, `crates/post/src/machine/def.rs`). `AxisLimits::turns_part` is now `Option<bool>`. A parsed rotary always states it (`true` for a table, `false` for a head tilt), so a rotary read back with no `turns_part` key was saved before #804. Opening such a project adds a load note through the existing load-note path (also logged), e.g. `the document's machine Tormach 770MX + microARC 4 was saved before rotaries stated whether they turn the work: rotary A is not judged by the index-swing check - reload the machine so it is`. Only rotaries on, or possibly on, the X line are named. No project version step is needed: the key stays additive, and a fresh save writes it.\n- **Tester kit README:** step 4 now says to keep the stored G30 Z at the top of travel, because the check assumes Z stays at the top while a `G30` turns A.\n- **Docs:** `assets/machines/AGENTS.md` documents the axis-vector rule. The decision record is `docs/decisions/813-swing-gaps.md`, covering what was chosen, what was dropped and why.\n\n## How tested\nNew tests in `crates/app/tests/index_swing.rs` (9 now, up from 6):\n- `a_custom_table_a_with_no_axis_vector_is_judged_about_x`: this is the shipped microARC text with the A's `axis` struck out.\n  - A too-long tool is refused by name, the post errs and nothing is written.\n  - A short tool clears.\n- `a_work_turning_rotary_with_no_line_and_no_rs274_letter_is_refused_by_name`: the rotary is renamed `U` and has no vector. The post is refused by name and nothing is written.\n- `a_project_saved_before_804_names_its_unjudged_table_a_on_load`:\n  - A fresh save opens with no note.\n  - The same save with `turns_part` stripped opens with one note, naming the machine and rotary A and asking for a reload.\n  - A machine with no rotary gets no note.\n\nThe existing tests now assert `turns_part == Some(true)` for the microARC and `Some(false)` for the tilt-head's rotaries.\n\n**Revert checks:**\n- With the letter fallback removed (an unstated vector read as off X), the two axis tests fail and the other seven pass.\n- With the note's `notes.extend` removed, the load test fails and the other eight pass.\n\nLocal results (macOS):\n\n| Suite | Result |\n|---|---|\n| index_swing | 9 passed |\n| tormach_fourth_axis | 12 passed |\n| microarc_test_part | 2 passed |\n| machine | 33 passed |\n| machine_library | 11 passed |\n| project_roundtrip | 71 passed |\n| conformance | 6 passed |\n| posting | 17 passed |\n| one_wcs | 8 passed |\n| tilt | 8 passed |\n| rotary_letter | 3 passed |\n| rotary_verify | 9 passed |\n| post_not_judged | 4 passed |\n| camcraft-post (all) | passed |\n\n`cargo clippy -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean. The Linux quick gate (`--base upstream/main`) is running; I'll comment its result.\n\n## Not exercised\n- **UI:** the load note was not seen in the GUI. It rides the existing `open_noted` / `load_noted` path that the #342 notes use, and that path is tested headlessly.\n- **A pre-#804 tilt-head project** gets the note too: the file can't tell a head tilt from a table A. Reloading clears it. This case isn't pinned by its own test.\n- **Hardware:** nothing has been run on a machine.\n\nChangelog: The A-index swing check also judges a work-turning A whose machine file states no axis vector (an A is about X), and refuses a work-turning rotary with neither a vector nor an A/B/C letter. A user's own table-A machine file that doesn't state `nose_to_axis` now has every operation refused by name until it does. Opening a project saved before the check names any embedded rotary it can't judge and asks for the machine to be reloaded.\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #813\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
